{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBirchSwinnertonDyer","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBirchSwinnertonDyer","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:QOU.Basic","lean:module:QOU.QuantumObservableUniverse.CategoricalFoundations","lean:module:Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Monoidal.Category","lean:module:Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Functor.Basic"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.FullQBSD_rank_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.FullQBSD_rank_zero","kind":"def","line":101,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBirchSwinnertonDyer.lean#L101-L115","doc":"**Full q-BSD formula** (r_q = 0 case) as a `Prop`:\n\nL(𝓔_q, 1) = Ω_q · |Ш_q(𝓔_q)| · ∏ c_𝔪(q)\n\nThe regulator and torsion terms collapse since the\nMordell–Weil group is trivial.\n\nRef: [birch1965] https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1965.218.79 ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.WeakQBSD","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.WeakQBSD","kind":"def","line":89,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBirchSwinnertonDyer.lean#L89-L99","doc":"**Weak q-BSD conjecture** as a `Prop`: for a given QOU, the\nL-function of the Descartes elliptic curve does not vanish at s = 1.\n\nAt the q-scale this is an unconditional theorem (Hasse–Weil).\nThe conjecture is that it persists through q → 1, predicting\nL(E₁, 1) ≠ 0 for E₁ : y² = x³ + ⅔x + 20/27.\n\nRef: [birch1965] https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1965.218.79\nRef: [wiles1995] https://doi.org/10.2307/2118559 ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.descartes_rank_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.descartes_rank_zero","kind":"theorem","line":68,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBirchSwinnertonDyer.lean#L68-L87","doc":"In a QOU where the elliptic fiber object `E_q` carries the fundamental\nrepresentation of `SU(2)`, the categorical Mordell–Weil group is trivial.\n\nThis follows from the observability axiom: `Hom(𝟙, Ad(A)) = 0` forces\n`G` semisimple, and `V_{1/2}^{SU(2)} = 0` for the fundamental.\n\nThe proof uses Tannaka–Krein: `Hom_𝐂(𝟙, E_q) ≅ τ(E_q)^G`, and the\nfact that the fundamental representation of any semisimple group has\nno invariant vectors.\n\nRef: [tate1974] https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01389745\n\nStated directly: `Hom(𝟙, Ad(A))` is a subsingleton by the\nadjoint_vanishing axiom. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.CategoricalMordellWeil","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.CategoricalMordellWeil","kind":"def","line":52,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBirchSwinnertonDyer.lean#L52-L66","doc":"The **categorical Mordell–Weil type** of an object `E` in a monoidal\ncategory: morphisms from the monoidal unit to `E`.\n\nThe group law (when it exists) is induced by the chord-tangent\naddition on the elliptic curve transported through the fibre functor.\nThis type may carry an `AddCommGroup` structure when `E` is an\nelliptic curve object; here we define only the underlying type.\n\nIn the QOU with `G = SU(2)`, `E = E_q` carries the fundamental\nrepresentation `V_{1/2}`.  Since `V_{1/2}^{SU(2)} = 0`, this\nHom-space is trivial (Subsingleton) in the Descartes QOU.\n\nLaTeX: `𝓔_q(𝟙) := Hom_𝐂(𝟙, E_q)` ","signatureChunk":0}]}